{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "We performed parallel review of titles, abstracts, and subsequently full texts based on updated inclusion and exclusion criteria following the population, exposure, comparison, outcome (PECO) approach (according to the latest PROSPERO protocol registered). We included children (defined as \u226418 years old) who were attending school, and their close contacts (family and household members, teachers, school support staff). We excluded home-schooled children and their close contacts, and schools with student numbers below 20. For study outcomes, we included infections traced to a school index case with a COVID-19 positive test. We updated inclusion criteria of study types to include: 1) Cohort studies: A. Prospective cohort study: contact tracing study where the exposed contacts are followed up and secondary infections are measured.... 2) Cross-sectional studies: A. Measurement of antibodies in a sero-surveillance study of schools at a point in time and then compared to background community rates at the same time. B. Measurement of active infection in all children/ staff in a school with PCR or antigen tests at a single point in time after schools open (within the first 14 days of opening) and then comparing the infection rate with age-adjusted community rates before schools opening. 3) Pre-post studies, where community rates of acute infection are compared for a period before schools opening and two or more weeks after schools opening.",
      "reason": "The chunk specifies the PECO framework, population (children ≤18 and contacts), exclusions (home-schooled, schools <20), eligible outcomes (infections traced to a school index case), and gives detailed eligible study designs and follow-up timing (e.g., follow-up of contacts, 14 days/ two or more weeks)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "We included articles in peer-reviewed journals and pre-prints, and excluded comments, conference abstracts and interviews.",
      "reason": "The review specifies publication/report status (peer-reviewed and pre-prints included; comments, abstracts, interviews excluded), but does not state any dissemination year limits or language restrictions in the provided evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "Five cross-sectional studies [19-23] from our 1st update that investigated SARS-COV-2 positivity but did not compare with community rates were excluded.",
      "reason": "The evidence shows studies were explicitly excluded because they did not compare school positivity with community rates (i.e., outcome comparison not measured), which indicates some distinction. However, there is no explicit statement contrasting exclusion due to outcomes not being measured versus exclusion because outcome results were not reported, so the distinction is only partially addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "Based on our new inclusion and exclusion criteria 12 studies were retained in our review: 1st update: n = 6 (cohort studies [13-17] and cross-sectional studies [18-23]; 2nd update: n = 6 (cohort studies [24-29]) (Figure 1) ... We found 11 cohort studies [13-17,24-29] that investigated secondary infection attack (Table 1 and Table 2) and one cross-sectional studies [18] that investigated SARS-CoV-2 positivity compared to community rates (Table 3 and Table 4).",
      "reason": "The review reports grouping studies into cohorts and cross-sectional categories and identifies which studies were included in the meta-analysis of infection attack rates, linking groups to the measured comparison (secondary infection attack / SARS-CoV-2 positivity vs community). However, it does not fully describe how those groups map to all specific comparisons defined in the review objectives in the provided text, so this is partial."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "Although notable restrictions (e.g., excluding home-schooled children, schools with <20 students, excluding household-only studies, excluding studies without community comparisons) are listed, the provided evidence does not give rationales explaining why these specific restrictions were applied."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 80
}

